\block{Why audit LLM recipes with flavor chemistry?}{
LLMs write recipes for millions of people and call them ``traditional''. Existing audits are textual; this lab's framework (Ahn et al.\ 2011; Jain, Rakhi \& Bagler 2015; Singh \& Bagler 2018) makes a cuisine's \textbf{culinary fingerprint} measurable and falsifiable. We score LLM cuisines with it, on all << F.regions.n >> CulinaryDB regions.
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{RQ1} Same food-pairing sign and strength? \textit{H1: pushed toward uniform pairing.}
\item \textbf{RQ2} Same composition and category fingerprint? \textit{H2b: SAI > 1; H2c: weaker fingerprint.}
\item \textbf{RQ3} Can fingerprint feedback steer generation without copying?
\item \textbf{RQ4} Do persona prompts, vendor or scale matter?
\end{itemize}
{\small\color{muted} Hypotheses and tests were pre-registered in the plan; one amendment (equal-size comparison) was logged before the analysis.}}

